\) is \\(125651\\).\n\nThis method provides a visual representation of the multiplication and can be very helpful for understanding the distributive property in a more concrete way. <|User|>That's very clear. Can you also show me how to solve \\(477 \\times 263\\) using the partial products method?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>.\nCertainly! The partial products method is another way to break down the multiplication into simpler parts. Here's how we can solve \\(477 \\times 263\\) using the partial products method:\n\n1. **Break down the numbers into their place values:**\n   - \\(477 = 400 + 70 + 7\\)\n   - \\(263 = 200 + 60 + 3\\)\n\n2. **Multiply each part of the first number by each part of the second number:**\n   - \\(400 \\times 200 = 80000\\)\n   - \\(400 \\times 60 = 24000\\)\n   - \\(400 \\times 3 = 1200\\)\n   - \\(70 \\times 200 = 14000\\)\n   - \\(70 \\times 60 = 4200\\)\n   - \\(70 \\times 3 = 210\\)\n   - \\(7 \\times 200 = 1400\\)\n   - \\(7 \\times 60 = 420\\)\n   - \\(7 \\times 3 = 21\\)\n\n3. **Add all the partial products together:**\n   \\[\n   80000 + 24000 + 1200 + 14000 + 4200 + 210 + 1400 + 420 + 21 = 125651\n   \\]\n\nSo, using the partial products method, the product of \\(477 \\times 263\\) is \\(125651\\).\n\nThis method involves breaking down the multiplication into smaller, more manageable parts and then summing those parts to get the final product. It's a systematic approach that can be very helpful for understanding the distributive property in a step-by-step manner. <|User|>Thank you for walking me through all these methods. Can you explain how to solve \\(477 \\times 263\\) using the lattice method?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>.\nCertainly! The lattice method is an alternative way to perform multiplication, especially useful for larger numbers. Here's how we can solve \\(477 \\times 263\\) using the lattice method:\n\n1. **Set up the lattice grid:**\n   - Draw a 3x3 grid (since \\(477\\) has 3 digits and \\(263\\) has 3 digits).\n   - Write the digits of \\(477\\) along the top of the grid and the digits of \\(263\\) along the right side of the grid.\n\n2. **Fill in the grid:**\n   - Multiply each digit of the top number by each digit of the right number, and write the result in the corresponding cell of the grid. If the result is a single digit, place it in the top-left corner of the cell; if it's a two-digit number, place the tens digit in the top-left corner and the units digit in the bottom-right corner.\n\nHere's the lattice grid setup for \\(477 \\times 263\\):\n\n```\n  4 | 7 | 7\n---------\n2 |   |   |\n6 |   |   |\n3 |   |   |\n---------\n```\n\n3. **Perform the multiplications:**\n   - Multiply 4 by each digit of 263:\n     - \\(4 \\times 2 = 8\\) (write 8 in the top-left corner)\n     - \\(4 \\times 6 = 24\\) (write 2 in the top-left corner and 4 in the bottom-right corner)\n     - \\(4 \\times 3 = 12\\) (write 1 in the top-left corner and 2 in the bottom-right corner)\n   - Multiply 7 by each digit of 263:\n     - \\(7 \\times 2 = 14\\) (write 1 in the top-left corner and 4 in the bottom-right corner)\n     - \\(7 \\times 6 = 42\\) (write 4 in the top-left corner and 2 in the bottom-right corner)\n     - \\(7 \\times 3 = 21\\) (write 2 in the top-left corner and 1 in the bottom-right corner)\n   - Multiply 7 by each digit of 263:\n     - \\(7 \\times 2 = 14\\) (write 1 in the top-left corner and 4 in the bottom-right corner)\n     - \\(7 \\times 6 = 42\\) (write 4 in the top-left corner and 2 in the bottom-right corner)\n     - \\(7 \\times 3 = 21\\) (write 2 in the top-left corner and 1 in the bottom-right corner)\n\n4. **Sum the diagonals:**\n   - Add the numbers in each diagonal, starting from the bottom right and moving to the top left.\n   - The diagonals will look like this:\n     - Bottom right: \\(4 + 2 + 1 = 7\\)\n     - Next diagonal: \\(2 + 4 + 2 = 8\\)\n     - Next diagonal: \\(1 + 2 + 1 = 4\\)\n     - Top left: \\(8\\)\n\n5. **Write the final product:**\n   - The numbers along 